### THE PUBLISHED-COMMIT KERNEL, REBUILT AND RUN BESIDE THE SHIPPED ONE
# Why: the rows this file used to hold were recorded at commit 05ce58f, before the
# response sweep's tail was rewritten. This benchmark source, compiled unchanged
# against that commit's headers, is the control -- same OpenCV, same compiler, same
# pinning, same machine, on 12 ALTERNATING launch pairs so drift divides out.
# SELECTION IDENTITY, checked before anything was timed. A speed comparison across a
# kernel change is meaningless if the answer moved. A probe built against BOTH header
# trees dumped, for each of the benchmark's four frames: an FNV-1a hash over the RAW BIT
# PATTERNS of the whole float response map, then every corner's index, x, y and response
# bits, for both spellings. 1454 lines each, byte-identical:
#   05ce58f md5 885940f0cef10ee6b30ac8189f2f87d8
#   shipped md5 885940f0cef10ee6b30ac8189f2f87d8
#   response-map bits per frame: 2d5619e775295694 c48fdabe99d226ae 8351d53b9a245547 5d6bb3ef693bd7a6
#   corners per frame: 184 174 184 181 (723 total), frame map and streaming ring alike
# The same probe on aarch64 produced the same md5, so the answer is identical across both
# kernels AND both architectures.
# 05ce58f kernel: binCV streaming median  14.561 ( 13.984- 16.016)   OpenCV binarized median  14.891   ratio 1.023
# shipped kernel: binCV streaming median  11.993 ( 11.757- 14.051)   OpenCV binarized median  13.727   ratio 1.145
# per-pair 05ce58f/shipped on binCV streaming: median 1.2090   min 0.9952   max 1.3470
